ying the  scene looking at what the americans have  left behind and trying to figure out how  do we operate this airport what skills  do we need what equipment do we need  what have we got on deck here that's  still functional that we can use we went  to the military side and the commercial  side and here's our report from there  we're on the military side of the  airport this is where the americans  vacated from last night you can still  see their planes equipment helicopters  here the taliban brought us here and  last night their mood was one of joy  celebration they were shooting in the  sky there were there were fireworks they  were very happy that the americans had  left the mood here today is quite  different they are disappointed they are  angry they say they feel betrayed  because  all of this equipment is broken beyond  repair they say that they expected the  americans to leave helicopters like this  in one piece for their use when i said  to them  why do you think that the americans  would have left everything operational  for you they said because we believe it  is a national asset and we are the  government now and this could have come  to great use for us so they are very  disappointed that a lot of this  equipment  cannot be used any longer  over to my left is where the commercial  side of the airport is  zabihullah mujahid who's a spokesman for  the taliban said that they hope to have  this area operational in the coming days  which would allow people who want to fly  out on commercial flights to do so with  visas so while the evacuation efforts  have finished because foreign forces  have all left  the taliban say that they are going to  stand by their word which is to allow  people to fly out commercially when they  get everything operational we understand  that turkey and qatar are assisting that  with them i can tell you that i saw  dozens of people arrive even to our own  hotel with equipment and personnel  ready to support the airport getting  back on its feet  engineers that type of thing  to try and get these these planes  running again but here on the military  side nothing will be operational any  time in the future the taliban  disappointed as this is what the  americans have left behind  well let's talk now to osama bin laden  who is in eastern afghanistan with  exclusive access to the city of  jalalabad so osama what is it like for  people on the taliban rule now  well just about a few minutes ago we  heard from taliban leaders who'd  gathered in the main square  they were telling people that the system  of corruption is over they were telling  people that there is going to be a  system of judicial justice they said  that now taliban will implement a system  which does not uh empower the powerful  but actually tries and go supports the  oppressed and this is promises that  we've heard from the taliban before but  this was different because the audience  with the taliban were speaking to were  people of jalalabad people who had  gathered from various locations and come  to the main ground and interestingly  enough just a few minutes after they  left now we can see a football team  playing as it was there's quite a lot of  people in the streets  and life continues to go on as normal in  jalalabad taliban came a little earlier  than the fall of kabul and people have  been accustomed to  taliban in the streets uh residents we  spoke to say that they are happy that  there is at least no lawlessness  there's no uh corruption on the streets  they're not being asked to pay bribes uh  on the checkpoints and it is  beginning to get a little better but  people are apprehensive because they  don't know and they still remember the  taliban from 20 years ago  and are the taliban clear on on how they  will rule afghanistan  well that is something that we've been  asking them and that is something that  people  are puzzled about as well because  there's a lot of angst about our taliban  going to ban uh all sorts of 